      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Certain patients with complex calcified left main (LM) disease have a prohibitive risk for bypass surgery. Rotational atherectomy (RA) prior to stent implantation is an option for this subset of patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To analyze acute and long-term results of RA in the LM location."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We present a single-center analysis of RA in severe LM disease applied in patients with high surgical risk."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "RA was performed in the LM location in 50 consecutive patients with a mean age of 73years. In 30% of the patients clinical presentation was an acute coronary syndrome, and 42% had diabetes. LM bifurcation was involved in 80% of the cases, 36% had a Medina class 1.1.1 lesion, and 38% of RA procedures were performed as bailout. In 38% of patients the left main was protected. Median logistic EuroSCORE was 12.4% (interquartile range, IQR, 5.24-36.11%) and mean SYNTAX Score was 28.6±8.2. The median burr size was 1.5mm and a two-stent strategy was required in 58% of interventions. Drug-eluting stents were implanted in 86% of procedures. Angiographic success rate was 96%, and in-hospital major adverse cardiac event rate was 10%. Survival free of cardiac death at 12 and 24months was 87.6% and 78.4%. Target lesion revascularization rates (TLR) were 13.3% and 18.8%, respectively. Cardiac deaths were significantly higher in patients with acute coronary syndromes compared with patients with stable angina (cardiac death free survival was 72.7% and 94% at 12months, p=0.01). The TLR rate was numerically higher in diabetic patients (21.1% vs. 7.7% at one year, p=0.18)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Acute and long-term outcomes after LM rotational atherectomy are satisfactory, considering the high procedure- and patient-related risks."
          }
        ]
      },
